WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Secrets management for shell scripts is a set of repeatable engineering habits.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.680
First, keep secrets out of scripts and repositories.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:15.660
A shell script can be powerful and still remain simple, but the secret itself should not become part of the script's permanent text.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:25.280
Once a credential is copied into a file, committed, shared, backed up, or built into an artifact, the cleanup problem becomes much harder than the original setup.

5
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.920
Second, map the flow of sensitive values.

6
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:38.460
Know where the secret enters, where it is stored, which process uses it, which child processes inherit it, what logs are produced, and where temporary material may persist.

7
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:50.320
This map helps teams find exposure paths that are easy to overlook, including environment dumps, command-line arguments, support bundles, artifacts, caches, shell history, and copied terminal sessions.

8
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.560
Third, use approved secret-management mechanisms or controlled runtime injection.

9
00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:07.740
Prefer managed secret stores, platform identity, short-lived credentials, CI/CD secret scopes, protected local files, or other approved channels over hard-coded credentials.

10
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:16.080
Check for required secrets without printing them, fail safely when they are missing, and keep logs useful without revealing sensitive values.

11
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.000
Finally, manage secrets over time.

12
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:30.860
Protect files and temporary data, minimize inheritance, keep build-time and runtime secrets separate, scope automation credentials tightly, and design scripts so rotation is practical.

13
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:38.560
Review legacy scripts, remove stale or shared credentials, and know how to revoke access quickly if exposure is suspected.

14
00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:46.980
The purpose is safe automation: scripts that can do their job without turning secrets into uncontrolled operational debris.
